# Python CLI Rules

## Code Style
- Python 3.10+ syntax (use `match` statements where appropriate)
- Use `pathlib.Path` — never `os.path` string manipulation
- Use `click` for CLI argument parsing (not argparse)
- Use type hints on all function signatures
- Use docstrings (Google style) on all public functions
- Line length: 100 characters max (per ruff config)

## Path Handling (Windows-First)
- NEVER hardcode absolute paths
- Use `pathlib.Path` for all path operations
- Use `/` separator in Path() — pathlib handles Windows conversion
- Read paths from `config/pipeline.yaml` when possible
- Use `Path(__file__).parent` for relative paths from script location

## CLI Conventions
- Every script must be runnable as `python scripts/python/script_name.py`
- Use `--input`, `--output`, `--config` as standard argument names
- Print progress to stdout
- Print errors to stderr
- Exit code 0 = success, 1 = error
- Support `--help` with clear descriptions
- Support `--verbose` for debug output

## Testing
- Every module in `scripts/python/` must have a test in `tests/`
- Test files named `test_*.py`
- Use `pytest` fixtures for common test data
- Test inputs in `tests/in/`, expected outputs in `tests/out_expected/`

## Do NOT
- Do not use `os.system()` or `subprocess.call()` for path operations
- Do not hardcode `C:\` or `D:\` paths
- Do not use `print()` for errors — use `click.echo(err=True)` or `logging`
- Do not catch and silence exceptions without logging
